(function(){"use strict";window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES=window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES||{};window.D_STUDY_TU101_LECTURES["l6"]={"id":"l6","lectureNumber":6,"lectureLabel":"Lecture 6","title":"Politics and Religion in Southeast Asia","theme":"Theme 2 — Southeast Asia in a Changing World","bankVersion":"tu101-l6-v13.16","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summary":[{"id":"tu6s1","titleEn":"1. History, Legend & Critical Reading","titleTh":"1. History, Legend และการอ่านอย่างวิพากษ์","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Evidence matters; every story has author + bias + context.","memoryTh":"จำ: ต้องดูหลักฐาน และดูผู้เขียน + bias + context","bullets":[{"en":"The lecture distinguishes <b>history as past events</b> from history as <b>evidence-based reconstruction by historians</b>.","th":"สไลด์แยก history ในฐานะเหตุการณ์อดีต กับ history ในฐานะ <b>การสร้างคำอธิบายจากหลักฐานโดยนักประวัติศาสตร์</b>"},{"en":"A <b>legend</b> is a long-transmitted story whose origin may be uncertain and which often explains people, places or origins.","th":"<b>Legend</b> คือเรื่องเล่าที่ส่งต่อมานาน ต้นกำเนิดอาจไม่ชัด และมักใช้อธิบายคน/สถานที่/ต้นกำเนิด"},{"en":"The professor’s truth ratios are a <b>teaching heuristic</b>, not a universal scientific formula; the point is to question how true and false become mixed.","th":"สัดส่วน true/false ในสไลด์เป็น <b>เครื่องมือสอน</b> ไม่ใช่สูตรสากล จุดสำคัญคือให้ถามว่าความจริง/เท็จถูกผสมอย่างไร"},{"en":"The slide names <b>author, bias, identity, wisdom and context</b> as filters shaping historical narratives.","th":"สไลด์ชี้ <b>ผู้เขียน bias identity wisdom context</b> ว่าเป็นตัวกรองเรื่องเล่าประวัติศาสตร์"}],"understandEn":"The exam lesson is not “history is false.” It is that historical knowledge must be evaluated through evidence, authorship and context rather than accepted as pure memory or patriotic story.","understandTh":"บทเรียนไม่ใช่ว่า history = เท็จ แต่ให้ประเมินผ่านหลักฐาน ผู้เขียน และบริบท ไม่เชื่อเรื่องเล่าเพราะคุ้นหรือรักชาติอย่างเดียว","diagram":["Evidence","Author","Bias / identity","Context"]},{"id":"tu6s2","titleEn":"2. Religion & Politics — Why They Intersect","titleTh":"2. Religion และ Politics — ทำไมจึงเชื่อมกัน","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Religion can legitimate, mobilize, resist and divide.","memoryTh":"จำ: ศาสนาให้ legitimacy • mobilize • ต่อต้าน • แบ่งขั้ว","bullets":[{"en":"Southeast Asia is religiously diverse: Buddhism, Islam, Christianity, Hinduism, Confucian/Taoist traditions and indigenous beliefs coexist across different regimes.","th":"เอเชียตะวันออกเฉียงใต้มีศาสนาหลากหลายทั้งพุทธ อิสลาม คริสต์ ฮินดู ขงจื๊อ/เต๋า และความเชื่อพื้นเมือง ในระบอบการเมืองต่างกัน"},{"en":"Religion can become a source of <b>national identity and political legitimacy</b>.","th":"ศาสนาสามารถเป็นแหล่ง <b>อัตลักษณ์ชาติและความชอบธรรมทางการเมือง</b>"},{"en":"Religious institutions and leaders can support governments, mediate crises, mobilize protest or organize resistance.","th":"สถาบัน/ผู้นำศาสนาอาจสนับสนุนรัฐ ไกล่เกลี่ยวิกฤต mobilize protest หรือจัดการต่อต้าน"},{"en":"Religious diversity can support pluralism but can also be politicized into identity conflict or majority/minority hierarchy.","th":"ความหลากหลายช่วย pluralism ได้ แต่ก็ถูก politicize ให้เป็นความขัดแย้งอัตลักษณ์/ลำดับ majority-minority ได้"}],"understandEn":"Religion is not treated only as private belief. The lecture studies it as an institution, identity and moral resource that affects authority, law, mobilization and social cohesion.","understandTh":"ศาสนาไม่ใช่แค่ความเชื่อส่วนตัว แต่เป็นสถาบัน อัตลักษณ์ และทรัพยากรทางศีลธรรมที่มีผลต่ออำนาจ กฎหมาย การระดมคน และ cohesion","diagram":["Identity","Legitimacy","Mobilization","Conflict / cohesion"]},{"id":"tu6s3","titleEn":"3. Ancient & Colonial Layers","titleTh":"3. ชั้นอิทธิพลยุคโบราณและอาณานิคม","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Indian + Chinese + Islamic + colonial layers.","memoryTh":"จำ: ชั้นอินเดีย + จีน + อิสลาม + อาณานิคม","bullets":[{"en":"Indian influence spread Hinduism and Buddhism through trade and cultural exchange, shaping kingdoms, Sanskrit traditions and temple-state legitimacy.","th":"อิทธิพลอินเดียส่งฮินดู/พุทธผ่านการค้า ส่งผลต่ออาณาจักร Sanskrit และ legitimacy ผ่านวัด/กษัตริย์"},{"en":"Chinese influence included tributary diplomacy, Confucian/Taoist/Buddhist traditions, governance models and migration.","th":"อิทธิพลจีนรวม tribute diplomacy ขงจื๊อ/เต๋า/พุทธ แบบการปกครอง และการอพยพ"},{"en":"Islam spread strongly through Arab, Indian and Malay trading networks from around the 13th century and became embedded in sultanates, law and scholarship.","th":"อิสลามแพร่ผ่านเครือข่ายพ่อค้า Arab/Indian/Malay ราวศตวรรษ 13 และฝังใน sultanate กฎหมาย และการศึกษา"},{"en":"Colonial powers used Christianity and religious administration to legitimize/control rule, while <b>divide-and-rule</b> could harden ethnic-religious boundaries.","th":"อาณานิคมใช้คริสต์/การจัดศาสนาเพื่อ legitimacy/control และ <b>divide-and-rule</b> อาจทำเส้นแบ่งชาติพันธุ์-ศาสนาแข็งขึ้น"},{"en":"At the same time, religion also became a resource for <b>anti-colonial resistance</b>.","th":"พร้อมกันนั้น ศาสนาก็เป็นทรัพยากรของ <b>anti-colonial resistance</b>"}],"understandEn":"Religion can serve opposite political functions depending on context. The same institution can be co-opted by rulers in one moment and mobilized against rulers in another.","understandTh":"ศาสนาทำหน้าที่การเมืองตรงข้ามกันได้ตามบริบท สถาบันเดียวอาจถูกรัฐดึงมาใช้ในเวลาหนึ่ง และกลายเป็นฐานต่อต้านในอีกเวลา","diagram":["Ancient legitimation","Colonial control","Divide & rule","Resistance"]},{"id":"tu6s4","titleEn":"4. Religion, Nationalism & Post-Independence States","titleTh":"4. ศาสนา Nationalism และรัฐหลังอิสรภาพ","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Faith can unify a nation — or define who is outside it.","memoryTh":"จำ: ศาสนารวมชาติได้ แต่ก็อาจกำหนดว่าใครถูกมองว่าอยู่นอกชาติ","bullets":[{"en":"Anti-colonial movements drew on Islamic organizations, Buddhist monks and Christian clergy to create moral legitimacy and solidarity.","th":"ขบวน anti-colonial ใช้องค์กรอิสลาม พระพุทธ และ clergy คริสต์เพื่อสร้าง moral legitimacy/solidarity"},{"en":"After independence, governments used religion to support <b>nation-building</b>, constitutional identity and political legitimacy.","th":"หลังอิสรภาพ รัฐใช้ศาสนาเพื่อ <b>nation-building</b> อัตลักษณ์รัฐธรรมนูญ และ legitimacy"},{"en":"Indonesia’s <b>Pancasila</b> tries to balance belief in God with plural religious citizenship rather than establish one official religious state.","th":"<b>Pancasila</b> ของ Indonesia พยายามสมดุลความเชื่อในพระเจ้ากับ pluralism ไม่ใช่รัฐศาสนาเดียว"},{"en":"Malaysia links Islam to the Federation and Malay politics; Brunei fuses monarchy, Malay identity and Islam; socialist Laos/Vietnam regulate religion more directly.","th":"Malaysia ผูกอิสลามกับ Federation/Malay politics; Brunei ผสาน monarchy-Malay-Islam; Laos/Vietnam สังคมนิยมควบคุมศาสนาเข้มกว่า"},{"en":"The recurring challenge is balancing majority identity with <b>minority rights and equal citizenship</b>.","th":"โจทย์ซ้ำคือสมดุล majority identity กับ <b>minority rights และ equal citizenship</b>"}],"understandEn":"Nation-building can use religion as social glue, but defining the nation through a majority faith can marginalize minorities. Political legitimacy and inclusion can therefore pull in different directions.","understandTh":"nation-building ใช้ศาสนาเป็นกาวสังคมได้ แต่ถ้านิยามชาติผ่านศาสนาคนส่วนใหญ่ อาจทำ minority ถูกกันออก legitimacy กับ inclusion จึงอาจตึงกัน","diagram":["Religion","Nation identity","Legitimacy","Minority rights"]},{"id":"tu6s5","titleEn":"5. Country Cases — Different Religion–State Arrangements","titleTh":"5. Country Cases — รูปแบบ Religion–State ต่างกัน","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: No single Southeast Asian religion–politics model.","memoryTh":"จำ: ไม่มีโมเดล religion-politics แบบเดียวทั้งภูมิภาค","bullets":[{"en":"<b>Indonesia:</b> Pancasila + pluralism + influential Muslim civil-society organizations and political-Islam debates.","th":"<b>Indonesia:</b> Pancasila + pluralism + องค์กรมุสลิม civil society + debate political Islam"},{"en":"<b>Malaysia:</b> Islam is constitutionally privileged and intertwined with Malay identity, UMNO/PAS competition and law/policy.","th":"<b>Malaysia:</b> Islam มีสถานะพิเศษ ผูก Malay identity และการแข่งขัน UMNO/PAS"},{"en":"<b>Thailand:</b> Buddhism is closely tied to national identity/monarchy while the Malay-Muslim southern border raises minority and conflict questions.","th":"<b>Thailand:</b> พุทธผูกกับชาติ/monarchy ขณะที่ชายแดนใต้ Malay-Muslim มีโจทย์ minority/conflict"},{"en":"<b>Philippines:</b> Catholic institutions influence public life; Moro Muslim grievances in Mindanao produced long autonomy struggles and BARMM.","th":"<b>Philippines:</b> Catholic มีอิทธิพลสาธารณะ; Moro Muslim ใน Mindanao ต่อสู้เรื่อง autonomy และมี BARMM"},{"en":"<b>Myanmar:</b> Buddhism can support democratic protest or nationalist exclusion; the Rohingya crisis shows religion intertwined with citizenship and ethnic hierarchy.","th":"<b>Myanmar:</b> พุทธอาจหนุน protest ประชาธิปไตยหรือ nationalist exclusion; Rohingya แสดงศาสนาผูก citizenship/ชาติพันธุ์"},{"en":"<b>Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam, Brunei, Timor-Leste</b> show further combinations of state Buddhism, socialist regulation, Islamic monarchy and Catholic nation-building.","th":"<b>Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam, Brunei, Timor-Leste</b> แสดงรูปแบบ state Buddhism, socialist control, Islamic monarchy และ Catholic nation-building"}],"understandEn":"Comparative questions should ask who controls religious institutions, how religion enters law/identity, whether minorities are protected, and whether religious actors support or challenge rulers.","understandTh":"เวลาเทียบประเทศ ให้ถามว่าใครควบคุมสถาบันศาสนา ศาสนาเข้า law/identity อย่างไร minority ได้รับการคุ้มครองไหม และ actor ศาสนาสนับสนุนหรือท้าทายรัฐ","diagram":["Plural compromise","State religion","State control","Religious civil society"]},{"id":"tu6s6","titleEn":"6. Religious Leaders as Political Actors","titleTh":"6. ผู้นำศาสนาในฐานะ Political Actors","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Moral authority becomes political leverage.","memoryTh":"จำ: moral authority เปลี่ยนเป็น political leverage","bullets":[{"en":"Religious leaders often have high social trust, giving them <b>moral authority</b> that can influence public decisions.","th":"ผู้นำศาสนามักได้รับความเชื่อถือสูง จึงมี <b>moral authority</b> ที่มีอิทธิพลการเมือง"},{"en":"Buddhist monks have participated in protest and nationalist movements; ulama shape debates on law/elections; Catholic clergy have mediated crises and supported democratization.","th":"พระพุทธเข้าร่วม protest/nationalism; ulama มีบทบาทต่อกฎหมาย/เลือกตั้ง; clergy Catholic ไกล่เกลี่ยและหนุน democratization"},{"en":"Religious actors can be <b>mediators, challengers or legitimizers</b> depending on their relationship with state power.","th":"actor ศาสนาอาจเป็น <b>mediator, challenger หรือ legitimizer</b> ตามความสัมพันธ์กับรัฐ"}],"understandEn":"Political power is not only formal office. Moral credibility, networks and community institutions can give religious leaders influence even without holding government positions.","understandTh":"อำนาจการเมืองไม่ได้มีแค่ตำแหน่งรัฐ ความน่าเชื่อถือ เครือข่าย และสถาบันชุมชนทำให้ผู้นำศาสนามีอิทธิพลได้แม้ไม่มี office","diagram":["Moral authority","Mobilize","Mediate","Legitimize / challenge"]},{"id":"tu6s7","titleEn":"7. Democracy, Populism & Future Dilemmas","titleTh":"7. Democracy, Populism และโจทย์อนาคต","memoryEn":"MEMORY HOOK: Religion can widen participation or narrow citizenship.","memoryTh":"จำ: ศาสนาเพิ่ม participation ได้ หรือทำ citizenship แคบลงก็ได้","bullets":[{"en":"Religion can support democracy through justice, community organization and civic mobilization—such as People Power or pro-democracy monastic activism.","th":"ศาสนาหนุน democracy ผ่าน justice ชุมชน และ civic mobilization เช่น People Power หรือพระเคลื่อนไหวประชาธิปไตย"},{"en":"But religious majoritarianism can restrict minority rights or pressure law to favor one identity.","th":"แต่ religious majoritarianism อาจจำกัด minority rights หรือผลักกฎหมายให้เอื้ออัตลักษณ์คนส่วนใหญ่"},{"en":"Populist leaders may use religious symbols and <b>good-versus-evil framing</b> to mobilize supporters, risking polarization.","th":"ผู้นำ populist อาจใช้สัญลักษณ์ศาสนา/กรอบ <b>good vs evil</b> ระดมผู้สนับสนุนและเพิ่ม polarization"},{"en":"Future dilemmas include religious nationalism, minority exclusion, transnational religious influences and balancing <b>pluralism, rights and stability</b>.","th":"โจทย์อนาคตรวม religious nationalism, minority exclusion, อิทธิพลศาสนาข้ามชาติ และสมดุล <b>pluralism, rights, stability</b>"}],"understandEn":"The lecture does not label religion as simply pro- or anti-democratic. Effects depend on institutions, majority/minority relations, political strategy and how religious authority is used.","understandTh":"บทไม่ได้บอกว่าศาสนา pro/anti democracy แบบตายตัว ผลขึ้นกับสถาบัน ความสัมพันธ์ majority-minority กลยุทธ์ และการใช้อำนาจศาสนา","diagram":["Civic action","Majoritarianism","Populism","Pluralism / rights"]}],"parts":{"tu6-history":{"label":"Part 1 • History, Legend & Religion–Politics Foundations","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summaries":["tu6s1","tu6s2"],"topics":["Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance"]},"tu6-history-religion":{"label":"Part 2 • Ancient, Colonial & Nationalist Religion","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summaries":["tu6s3","tu6s4"],"topics":["Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance"]},"tu6-cases":{"label":"Part 3 • Country Cases: Religion & State","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summaries":["tu6s5","tu6s6"],"topics":["Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control"]},"tu6-democracy":{"label":"Part 4 • Democracy, Populism, Minority Rights & State Control","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summaries":["tu6s6","tu6s7"],"topics":["Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance"]},"tu6-all":{"label":"Cumulative • Lecture 6","source":"Professor Chaiwat politics/religion slide (primary) + assigned religion-politics references (support)","summaries":["tu6s1","tu6s2","tu6s3","tu6s4","tu6s5","tu6s6","tu6s7"],"topics":null,"cumulative":true}},"review":{"tu6-history":[{"q":"What two meanings of history does the lecture distinguish?","opts":["Past events themselves and historians’ evidence-based reconstruction of those events","Only legends and myths","Only government textbooks","Future predictions and law"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The opening slide separates what happened from researched historical accounts."},{"q":"What is a legend in the lecture definition?","opts":["A long-transmitted story with uncertain origins that often explains people, places or beginnings","A peer-reviewed statistical table","A current law","A scientific experiment"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The Royal Institute definition emphasizes inherited narrative over time."},{"q":"How should the slide’s 7/3 and 3/7 truth ratios be understood?","opts":["As a classroom heuristic encouraging skepticism, not a universal measured law","As scientifically proven percentages for every history book","As constitutional rules","As exact probability formulas"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The pedagogical point is mixing of true/false, not a universal empirical ratio."},{"q":"Which factors does the lecture say shape historical narratives?","opts":["Author, bias, identity, wisdom and context","Only climate and geography","Only election results","Only language grammar"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"These five are displayed as causes of mixing true and false."},{"q":"What is bias in the slide?","opts":["Prejudice or partiality that shapes judgment","A neutral archive","A religious institution","A territorial boundary"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The slide links bias to love, anger, fear and ignorance."},{"q":"Why is Bang Rachan used in the lecture?","opts":["To show how heroic national narratives can be questioned through source comparison and later state promotion","To prove every national hero is fictional","To teach only military tactics","To show legends never affect politics"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The case contrasts familiar patriotic story with questions about evidence and later representation."},{"q":"What best describes Southeast Asia’s religious landscape?","opts":["Highly diverse, with major Buddhist, Muslim, Christian and other traditions across different states","Religiously uniform","Entirely secular","Only Buddhist"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The lecture maps multiple major and local religious traditions."},{"q":"How can religion provide political legitimacy?","opts":["Leaders can connect authority to sacred values, institutions or majority identity","Religion legally guarantees competence","Only elected leaders use legitimacy","Religious legitimacy eliminates opposition"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"The lecture repeatedly shows rulers using religion as a source of moral authority."},{"q":"What dual political role can religious diversity play?","opts":["It can support pluralist cohesion or become a basis for identity conflict","It only causes war","It only increases democracy","It has no effect on politics"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The slide emphasizes both strength and challenges."},{"q":"Why does the course reject treating religion as purely private belief?","opts":["Religious institutions and identities affect authority, law, mobilization and public legitimacy","Because all states are theocracies","Because private belief is illegal","Because religion always controls government"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"The lecture studies religious actors as political participants."}],"tu6-history-religion":[{"q":"How did Indian religious influence enter early Southeast Asia?","opts":["Through trade, migration and cultural exchange that spread Hindu-Buddhist ideas and statecraft","Only through modern military conquest","Through European colonial schools","After 1945 only"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"The slide links Indianized kingdoms to trade and religious-cultural transmission."},{"q":"What did Chinese influence include?","opts":["Tributary relations, Confucian/Taoist/Buddhist traditions, governance ideas and migration","Only Roman Catholic missions","No political interaction","Only modern tourism"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"The lecture presents both state relations and cultural migration."},{"q":"How did Islam spread strongly into Southeast Asia according to the lecture?","opts":["Through Arab, Indian and Malay trade networks from around the 13th century","Only through European colonial armies","Only after World War II","By banning maritime commerce"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Trade and sultanates are central to the slide’s Islamization story."},{"q":"How did European colonial powers use religion?","opts":["They used missions and religious institutions to legitimize rule, educate populations and consolidate control","They universally banned Christianity","They ignored religion entirely","They transferred sovereignty to monks"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Christian missions and governance are part of the colonial section."},{"q":"What is divide-and-rule in the religion/colonial context?","opts":["Colonial authorities govern partly by emphasizing or institutionalizing ethnic/religious differences","A policy unifying all groups under equal institutions","A trade theory","A monastic meditation method"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"The slide says colonial regimes sometimes privileged groups and hardened categories."},{"q":"How did religion also work against colonial rule?","opts":["Religious leaders and communities supplied solidarity, legitimacy and networks for resistance","Religion always supported colonial governments","Anti-colonialism was purely secular everywhere","Religious networks were politically inactive"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"The slide cites Catholic, Islamic and Buddhist anti-colonial examples."},{"q":"Which Islamic organizations are highlighted in Indonesian anti-colonial/national politics?","opts":["Sarekat Islam, Muhammadiyah and Nahdlatul Ulama","PAP, UMNO and CPP","NATO, WTO and ASEAN","MNLF only"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"The lecture names these as important Islamic social/political organizations."},{"q":"Where does the lecture highlight Buddhist participation in anti-colonial nationalism?","opts":["Burma/Myanmar and Vietnam","Only Brunei","Only the Philippines","Only Singapore"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Monks/networks are cited in resistance to British/French rule."},{"q":"What anti-colonial role did Catholicism play in the lecture?","opts":["Clergy and church networks supported resistance in the Philippines and later Timor-Leste nation-building","It only legitimized Spanish rule","It was absent from Southeast Asia","It ended all nationalist movements"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"The slide presents Christianity as both colonial legacy and resistance resource."},{"q":"How did post-independence states use religion?","opts":["As a resource for national identity, legitimacy and social cohesion, while regulating it in different ways","They all became secular in identical ways","They universally banned religion","They surrendered government to clergy"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Country models range from religious states to plural compromise and socialist regulation."}],"tu6-cases":[{"q":"What role does Pancasila play in Indonesia’s religion-state relationship?","opts":["It provides a plural state ideology recognizing belief in God without making one religion the sole state faith","It establishes an atheist state","It makes Indonesia an Islamic monarchy","It bans religious organizations"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The lecture treats Pancasila as a compromise for religious diversity."},{"q":"What do Nahdlatul Ulama and Muhammadiyah illustrate in Indonesia?","opts":["Large Islamic organizations can be major civil-society and political influences","Only government ministries","Military factions","Foreign embassies"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"They shape social life and debates on political Islam."},{"q":"How is Islam positioned in Malaysia?","opts":["Islam is the religion of the Federation and is closely intertwined with Malay identity and party politics","Religion is constitutionally banned","Malaysia has an atheist constitution","Islam has no role in law or education"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The lecture connects Islam with UMNO/PAS and Malay politics."},{"q":"What religion-state pattern does Thailand illustrate?","opts":["Buddhist-majority national identity with formal religious freedom and a politically important Malay-Muslim southern border","Islamic absolute monarchy","One-party atheist state","Catholic republic"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The slide ties Buddhism to nation/monarchy and highlights southern-border pluralism."},{"q":"What dual pattern defines religion and politics in the Philippines?","opts":["Strong Catholic influence nationally alongside long Muslim Moro struggles for recognition/autonomy in Mindanao","No religious influence in politics","Buddhist state religion only","One uniform Christian identity with no minority issue"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The lecture contrasts Catholic public power with Moro conflict/autonomy."},{"q":"How can the Catholic Church influence Philippine politics without governing directly?","opts":["Through moral authority, public advocacy, mediation and mobilization","By appointing every president","By owning the armed forces","Only through tax collection"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"The lecture notes church roles in policy debates and political crises."},{"q":"What makes Buddhism politically complex in Myanmar?","opts":["Monks and Buddhist identity have supported both pro-democracy activism and exclusionary nationalism","Buddhism has no political role","All monks support identical politics","Myanmar is a secular state with no religion"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The slide contrasts Saffron Revolution with nationalist movements."},{"q":"What political issue is central to the Rohingya case?","opts":["A Muslim minority faces discrimination/statelessness where religion, ethnicity and citizenship exclusion overlap","A dispute over Catholic education only","A tariff conflict","A language reform with no rights dimension"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The lecture uses Rohingya to show the danger of religious-nationalist exclusion."},{"q":"What is Brunei’s Melayu Islam Beraja (MIB)?","opts":["Malay Islamic Monarchy—an ideology fusing Malay identity, Islam and royal authority","A secular republican constitution","A communist party doctrine","A regional trade pact"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"MIB is the official ideological foundation highlighted in the lecture."},{"q":"How do Laos and Vietnam broadly manage religion?","opts":["They tolerate recognized religious practice under substantial socialist state supervision and regulation","They make clergy sovereign rulers","They have no religious communities","They guarantee complete institutional independence from the state"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"The slide describes religion as allowed but subordinated to national/socialist objectives."}],"tu6-democracy":[{"q":"What does the lecture mean by a spectrum from secular to religious politics?","opts":["States vary in how directly religion is embedded in law, identity and governance","Every state is either perfectly secular or a theocracy","Religion only matters in monarchies","Secularism means banning all faith"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The lecture compares Singapore/socialist regulation, hybrid models and religiously embedded states."},{"q":"What is secular politics in the lecture’s ideal-type definition?","opts":["State neutrality or institutional separation between religious authority and political rule","Mandatory atheism in every case","Rule by clerics","Only Buddhist government"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The slide contrasts secular and religious politics conceptually."},{"q":"How can religion support democratization?","opts":["Religious values, networks and leaders can mobilize justice, participation and resistance to authoritarianism","Religion always eliminates elections","Only secular groups can mobilize citizens","Faith has no relation to civil society"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"People Power and Saffron examples show pro-democracy religious mobilization."},{"q":"What is religious majoritarianism?","opts":["Using the majority faith as a basis for privileging one political identity over minorities","Equal treatment of all beliefs","Complete privatization of religion","Minority autonomy by definition"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The lecture warns that majority religion can narrow plural citizenship."},{"q":"How do populist leaders use religion?","opts":["They invoke religious symbols and moral language to claim closeness with 'the people'","They always remove identity from politics","They avoid good/evil framing","They only discuss monetary policy"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The slide identifies religious symbolism as a populist mobilization tool."},{"q":"What is a risk of religious populism?","opts":["Polarization and weakening of pluralism/minority rights","Automatic consensus","End of identity politics","Guaranteed institutional moderation"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The lecture warns of deepened social divisions."},{"q":"What recurring challenge links Thailand’s south, Rohingya and Moro cases?","opts":["Balancing majority national identity with minority recognition, rights and political inclusion","Only international trade","Only climate change","No citizenship questions"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Each case connects religion with minority identity and state membership."},{"q":"What future influence can reshape domestic religious politics?","opts":["Transnational religious movements and geopolitical networks","Only village boundaries","The disappearance of communication","No external ideas"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The lecture mentions Middle East Islamism, evangelical networks and Buddhist nationalism."},{"q":"What is the central future dilemma in the lecture conclusion?","opts":["Balancing religious identity, pluralism, rights and political stability","Choosing one religion for all ASEAN states","Ending every religious institution","Eliminating national governments"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The conclusion emphasizes unity/division and rights/stability trade-offs."},{"q":"What is the best overall conclusion about religion’s political effect?","opts":["It is not fixed: religion can legitimate rulers, resist them, support democracy or fuel exclusion depending on context","Religion is always democratic","Religion is always authoritarian","Religion has no political effects"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"The entire lecture demonstrates context-dependent political uses."}]},"mastery":{"tu6-history":[{"q":"Why is this distinction important for critical reading?","opts":["The past cannot be accessed directly; historical accounts are selected and constructed from surviving evidence","It proves all history is invented","Evidence has no role in history","Only eyewitness memory is valid"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Historical knowledge is mediated by sources and interpretation."},{"q":"Why should a legend not automatically be discarded as useless?","opts":["It can reveal values, identity and collective memory even when its factual content needs verification","Every legend is literally true","Legends replace archival evidence","Legends have no social effects"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Narratives matter culturally while factual claims still require evidence."},{"q":"A student says 'all history is exactly 70% true.' What is wrong?","opts":["They turned an illustrative teaching device into a universal empirical claim","Nothing; the ratio is a natural law","History is always 30% true instead","Only legends can be evaluated"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"The lecture uses the ratio to provoke critical thinking."},{"q":"Two historians use the same event but reach different interpretations. Which explanation best fits the lecture?","opts":["Different perspectives, identities, assumptions and contexts can shape selection and interpretation of evidence","One must necessarily be lying","Evidence has no meaning","All interpretations are equally factual"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Interpretation varies without making evidence irrelevant."},{"q":"Why is recognizing bias not the same as rejecting a source?","opts":["Bias is one feature to evaluate alongside evidence, context and corroboration","Any biased source contains zero useful information","Only unbiased people create history","Bias can be measured by one number"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Critical method evaluates rather than automatically discards."},{"q":"What broader skill does the Bang Rachan case teach?","opts":["Separate the social importance of a national story from the strength of evidence for each historical claim","Reject all patriotic narratives without research","Accept textbooks as final authority","Ignore context of later commemoration"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"A narrative can be politically powerful even when parts are contested."},{"q":"Why does religious diversity matter politically?","opts":["It shapes identity, law, legitimacy, coalition-building and minority-majority relations","Religion never enters public policy","All religions produce identical institutions","Diversity automatically causes conflict"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Religious identity can be a political resource and cleavage."},{"q":"Why might temple sponsorship or religious ceremony help a ruler politically?","opts":["It publicly associates the ruler with respected moral traditions and community institutions","It automatically changes the constitution","It abolishes economic grievances","It proves religious leaders control the state"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Symbolic association can strengthen perceived legitimacy."},{"q":"What determines whether diversity becomes conflict?","opts":["Institutions, political mobilization, rights protection and how identities are framed","Diversity itself mechanically causes violence","Only theology matters","Economic policy has no interaction"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religious and political diversity in Southeast Asia","why":"Political context mediates identity effects."},{"q":"A priest holds no office but can mobilize thousands and mediate a crisis. What kind of power is this?","opts":["Informal political influence based on moral authority and social networks","Formal executive power","Military sovereignty","No political power at all"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Political influence exists beyond state office."}],"tu6-history-religion":[{"q":"Why did rulers adopt Hindu-Buddhist symbols and temple architecture?","opts":["They could use cosmology and sacred traditions to legitimize kingship and political order","Temples automatically created democracy","Religion had no relation to authority","Only merchants could use religious symbols"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Religion supported statecraft and legitimacy."},{"q":"Why were tributary relations politically useful to Southeast Asian kingdoms?","opts":["They could secure trade, recognition and relations with a powerful regional court","They made kingdoms Chinese provinces automatically","They ended local sovereignty in every case","They banned maritime trade"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Tribute could be a diplomatic-economic relationship rather than simple annexation."},{"q":"Why could port cities become centers of Islamization?","opts":["Trade networks linked merchants, scholars and rulers, making religious affiliation useful socially and commercially","Ports were isolated from foreigners","Islam required inland agriculture only","Maritime trade prevented cultural exchange"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Commercial networks also transmit ideas and institutions."},{"q":"Why can missionary education have long-term political effects?","opts":["Schools shape literacy, elites, identity and networks that may later support either colonial administration or nationalism","Education has no political consequences","Mission schools only teach ritual","Colonial states cannot create local elites"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Institutions can outlast their original political purpose."},{"q":"Why can colonial classification create postcolonial problems?","opts":["Administrative categories can harden fluid identities into durable political divisions and unequal access","Categories disappear automatically at independence","Colonial censuses have no social effects","Identity cannot be shaped institutionally"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Institutions can make distinctions politically consequential."},{"q":"Why can the same religion support both rule and resistance?","opts":["Political effects depend on which actors control institutions and how sacred authority is interpreted in context","Religious doctrines mechanically dictate one politics","Colonial governments and rebels share identical interests","Religion has no institutions"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Religion is a resource used differently by competing actors."},{"q":"What does their role show about religious organizations?","opts":["They can combine social services, community identity and political mobilization rather than operate only as worship groups","Religious organizations cannot enter public life","Only states provide social networks","Anti-colonial movements have no institutional base"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Organizational capacity makes religion politically consequential."},{"q":"Why can monastic networks mobilize nationalism?","opts":["They possess trusted institutions, communication networks and moral authority embedded in local communities","Monks control all armies","Nationalism requires religious office","Temples cannot communicate politically"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Institutional reach supports collective action."},{"q":"Why did Catholic identity become especially important in Timor-Leste?","opts":["Church institutions provided protection and solidarity during occupation, linking faith to national resistance","Catholicism was imposed by ASEAN","Religion had no role during occupation","It eliminated national identity"],"ans":0,"topic":"Colonialism, nationalism, and post-independence nation-building","why":"Shared faith became part of a political community under pressure."},{"q":"What is the core nation-building dilemma when majority religion becomes a state symbol?","opts":["It may strengthen common identity while making minorities feel less equal or less included","State symbols never affect citizenship","Minorities automatically adopt the majority faith","Religious unity eliminates political conflict"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Unity and inclusion can pull in different directions."}],"tu6-cases":[{"q":"Why is Pancasila a hybrid rather than purely secular or theocratic model?","opts":["The state affirms religious belief as foundational while preserving multiple recognized faith communities","It separates religion completely from public life","It gives clerics direct sovereign rule","It recognizes no religion"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"It combines religious affirmation with plural citizenship."},{"q":"Why can such organizations moderate as well as mobilize religious politics?","opts":["They have institutional networks capable of bargaining, education and community service—not only protest","Civil-society organizations have no policy influence","Religious groups always radicalize politics","Only parties can mediate interests"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"Organizational capacity supports multiple political roles."},{"q":"Why can UMNO–PAS competition intensify religious policy appeals?","opts":["Parties competing for Malay-Muslim voters have incentives to demonstrate religious credibility","Parties cannot mention religion","Malay identity is unrelated to Islam","Electoral competition always secularizes politics"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"Identity-based constituencies shape party strategies."},{"q":"Why is the southern border not simply a religious conflict?","opts":["Religion interacts with history, Malay identity, territory and governance grievances","Only theology matters","There are no identity issues","State policy has no effect"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"Political conflicts usually combine multiple identity and governance dimensions."},{"q":"What does BARMM represent in this context?","opts":["An institutional autonomy arrangement responding to long-standing Moro political and identity grievances","A Catholic church council","A national military dictatorship","A trade agreement"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"Autonomy can address minority self-government within the state."},{"q":"Why did Church involvement matter during People Power?","opts":["Religious legitimacy and networks helped coordinate and morally frame mass opposition","The Church held constitutional executive power","No citizens participated","It was purely a private worship event"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Moral institutions can lower coordination barriers and legitimize protest."},{"q":"What lesson follows from these opposite Buddhist political roles?","opts":["A religion has no fixed political effect; institutions and actors can deploy it for different agendas","Buddhism is inherently democratic","Buddhism is inherently authoritarian","Religious doctrine alone predicts politics"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"Political context determines how religious identity is mobilized."},{"q":"Why is statelessness especially severe for minority rights?","opts":["Without recognized citizenship, people can lack legal protection, political membership and access to state rights","Citizenship has no relation to rights","Statelessness grants extra representation","It affects only religious ritual"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Legal membership is a gateway to many protections."},{"q":"Why is Brunei near the religiously embedded end of the regional spectrum?","opts":["The Sultan combines political and religious authority and Islamic law is integrated into governance","Religion is excluded from state identity","All religious authority is independent of government","The monarchy is purely ceremonial"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"State, monarchy and religion are institutionally intertwined."},{"q":"Why might a socialist government present Buddhism as cultural heritage while regulating independent organization?","opts":["It can embrace identity/tradition while limiting autonomous institutions that might become alternative political centers","Culture and politics can never be separated","Religious institutions have no networks","State supervision always increases clerical autonomy"],"ans":0,"topic":"Country cases: religion and politics across Southeast Asia","why":"States can distinguish cultural religion from independent political organization."}],"tu6-democracy":[{"q":"Why are most Southeast Asian systems better understood as hybrids than pure endpoints?","opts":["Even secular-leaning states regulate religion, while religiously identified states still use modern constitutional/bureaucratic institutions","Hybrid means no political rules","Religion and state are always completely separate","All states use identical models"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Real arrangements combine different degrees of separation and incorporation."},{"q":"Why is state control of religion not exactly the same as liberal secular neutrality?","opts":["A state may be non-theocratic yet actively regulate religious organizations rather than remain neutral","Any regulation equals theocracy","Secular states cannot make laws","Neutrality means state religion"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Separation, neutrality and control are distinct arrangements."},{"q":"What makes religious networks useful in authoritarian settings?","opts":["Existing trusted institutions can organize people when formal political channels are weak or restricted","They possess automatic legal immunity","They always control the military","They have no social trust"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Networks and legitimacy can substitute for restricted political organization."},{"q":"Why can majoritarian religious politics weaken democracy even if supported by a voting majority?","opts":["Democracy also requires equal rights and protections for minorities, not just majority preference","Any majority decision is automatically liberal","Minority rights are unrelated to democracy","Elections require religious uniformity"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Liberal democracy limits majority power through rights."},{"q":"Why is 'good people versus evil enemies' framing politically powerful?","opts":["It converts policy disagreement into moral identity conflict and can intensify loyalty/polarization","It encourages nuanced compromise automatically","It removes group boundaries","It prevents charismatic leadership"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Moralization raises emotional stakes."},{"q":"Why can polarization weaken democratic institutions?","opts":["Opponents may be treated as illegitimate enemies, making compromise and neutral rules harder to sustain","Polarization guarantees independent courts","It makes all groups trust each other","It removes executive power"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Democracy requires acceptance of legitimate opposition."},{"q":"Which policy goal best addresses the common democratic problem across these cases?","opts":["Protect equal citizenship while creating legitimate channels for cultural/religious recognition and political voice","Force complete cultural assimilation","Remove all local representation","Treat every minority claim as foreign intervention"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Inclusion reduces the zero-sum identity problem."},{"q":"Why does globalization complicate national regulation of religion?","opts":["Ideas, funding and organizations can cross borders faster than purely domestic institutions can contain them","Globalization makes religion disappear","All religious authority becomes governmental","Borders prevent all communication"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"Transnational networks link domestic identity to global movements."},{"q":"Which approach best fits the lecture’s comparative lesson?","opts":["Build institutions that allow religious participation while protecting equal citizenship and preventing coercive exclusion","Ban religion from society entirely","Let the majority faith override all rights","Assume diversity manages itself without institutions"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion, democracy, populism, minority rights, and state control","why":"The course seeks institutional balance rather than one universal religion-state model."},{"q":"The same Buddhist institution supports protest in one period and nationalist state policy in another. What should a student infer?","opts":["Analyze actors, institutions and context rather than assigning a permanent political essence to the religion","One of the cases must be impossible","Religious doctrine fully determines one political outcome","Historical context is irrelevant"],"ans":0,"topic":"Religion as political legitimacy, governance, and resistance","why":"Political function varies by who mobilizes religion and for what purpose."}]},"cumulativeId":"tu6-all","flashcards":{"id":"tu101-l6","label":"Lecture 6 • Politics and Religion in Southeast Asia","description":"History and myth, religion as legitimacy/resistance, country cases, democracy, populism and minority rights.","cumulativeId":"tu101-l6-full-v1","cumulativeLabel":"Full Lecture • Lecture 6","parts":[{"id":"tu6-l6-history-v1","label":"Part 1 • History, Legend & Religion–Politics Foundations","description":"Critical source reading, bias, identity and the basic religion-politics framework.","cards":[{"id":"history-two","front":"What two meanings of history does the lecture distinguish?","back":"Past events themselves and historians’ evidence-based reconstruction of those events ไทยช่วยจำ: อดีตจริงกับคำอธิบายของนักประวัติศาสตร์ไม่ใช่สิ่งเดียวกัน"},{"id":"legend","front":"What is a legend in the lecture definition?","back":"A long-transmitted story with uncertain origins that often explains people, places or beginnings ไทยช่วยจำ: legend อาจไม่แม่น facts แต่บอก identity/memory ของสังคมได้"},{"id":"truth-ratio","front":"How should the slide’s 7/3 and 3/7 truth ratios be understood?","back":"As a classroom heuristic encouraging skepticism, not a universal measured law ไทยช่วยจำ: เป็น heuristic ไม่ใช่สูตรวิทยาศาสตร์"},{"id":"filters","front":"Which factors does the lecture say shape historical narratives?","back":"Author, bias, identity, wisdom and context ไทยช่วยจำ: หลักฐานเดียวกันอาจถูกอ่านต่างตามผู้เขียน/บริบท"},{"id":"bias","front":"What is bias in the slide?","back":"Prejudice or partiality that shapes judgment ไทยช่วยจำ: รู้ว่า source มี bias แล้วตรวจเทียบ ไม่ใช่ทิ้งอัตโนมัติ"},{"id":"bangrachan","front":"Why is Bang Rachan used in the lecture?","back":"To show how heroic national narratives can be questioned through source comparison and later state promotion ไทยช่วยจำ: แยกพลังของเรื่องเล่ากับความแข็งของหลักฐาน"},{"id":"religion-div","front":"What best describes Southeast Asia’s religious landscape?","back":"Highly diverse, with major Buddhist, Muslim, Christian and other traditions across different states ไทยช่วยจำ: ความหลากหลายศาสนากระทบ identity/กฎหมาย/majority-minority"},{"id":"legit","front":"How can religion provide political legitimacy?","back":"Leaders can connect authority to sacred values, institutions or majority identity ไทยช่วยจำ: ผู้นำเชื่อมตนกับศาสนาที่คนเคารพเพื่อเพิ่ม legitimacy"},{"id":"cohesion-conflict","front":"What dual political role can religious diversity play?","back":"It can support pluralist cohesion or become a basis for identity conflict ไทยช่วยจำ: ความหลากหลายไม่ทำให้ conflict อัตโนมัติ; สถาบัน/การ mobilize สำคัญ"},{"id":"private-public","front":"Why does the course reject treating religion as purely private belief?","back":"Religious institutions and identities affect authority, law, mobilization and public legitimacy ไทยช่วยจำ: ไม่มีตำแหน่งรัฐก็มี political influence ผ่าน moral authority/network ได้"}]},{"id":"tu6-l6-historical-v1","label":"Part 2 • Ancient, Colonial & Nationalist Religion","description":"Religious diffusion, colonial governance, anti-colonial resistance and nation-building.","cards":[{"id":"indian","front":"How did Indian religious influence enter early Southeast Asia?","back":"Through trade, migration and cultural exchange that spread Hindu-Buddhist ideas and statecraft ไทยช่วยจำ: รับศาสนา/สัญลักษณ์เพื่อเสริม legitimacy ของกษัตริย์และรัฐ"},{"id":"chinese","front":"What did Chinese influence include?","back":"Tributary relations, Confucian/Taoist/Buddhist traditions, governance ideas and migration ไทยช่วยจำ: tribute เชื่อม trade/recognition กับอำนาจจีนโดยไม่เท่ากับถูก annex ทุกกรณี"},{"id":"islam","front":"How did Islam spread strongly into Southeast Asia according to the lecture?","back":"Through Arab, Indian and Malay trade networks from around the 13th century ไทยช่วยจำ: เมืองท่าเชื่อม merchant/scholar/ruler จึงแพร่ศาสนาได้ดี"},{"id":"colonial-use","front":"How did European colonial powers use religion?","back":"They used missions and religious institutions to legitimize rule, educate populations and consolidate control ไทยช่วยจำ: โรงเรียน mission สร้าง elite/network ที่ภายหลังอาจหนุนหรือท้าทายอาณานิคม"},{"id":"divide","front":"What is divide-and-rule in the religion/colonial context?","back":"Colonial authorities govern partly by emphasizing or institutionalizing ethnic/religious differences ไทยช่วยจำ: รัฐอาณานิคมทำ category แข็งขึ้นจน division อยู่ต่อหลังอิสรภาพ"},{"id":"resistance","front":"How did religion also work against colonial rule?","back":"Religious leaders and communities supplied solidarity, legitimacy and networks for resistance ไทยช่วยจำ: ศาสนาเป็น resource ที่รัฐหรือฝ่ายต่อต้านก็ใช้ได้ตามบริบท"},{"id":"islam-anticolonial","front":"Which Islamic organizations are highlighted in Indonesian anti-colonial/national politics?","back":"Sarekat Islam, Muhammadiyah and Nahdlatul Ulama ไทยช่วยจำ: องค์กรศาสนาเป็นทั้ง community/social service และฐาน mobilization"},{"id":"buddhist-nationalism","front":"Where does the lecture highlight Buddhist participation in anti-colonial nationalism?","back":"Burma/Myanmar and Vietnam ไทยช่วยจำ: วัด/พระมี network และ moral authority ในชุมชน"},{"id":"catholic","front":"What anti-colonial role did Catholicism play in the lecture?","back":"Clergy and church networks supported resistance in the Philippines and later Timor-Leste nation-building ไทยช่วยจำ: Church เป็นพื้นที่ปกป้อง/solidarity จึงผูกกับ national identity"},{"id":"postind","front":"How did post-independence states use religion?","back":"As a resource for national identity, legitimacy and social cohesion, while regulating it in different ways ไทยช่วยจำ: majority religion ช่วย unity แต่เสี่ยงทำ minority รู้สึกเป็นคนนอก"}]},{"id":"tu6-l6-cases-v1","label":"Part 3 • Country Cases: Religion & State","description":"Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, Philippines, Myanmar and other regional models.","cards":[{"id":"pancasila","front":"What role does Pancasila play in Indonesia’s religion-state relationship?","back":"It provides a plural state ideology recognizing belief in God without making one religion the sole state faith ไทยช่วยจำ: ยอมรับ belief in God แต่ไม่ตั้งศาสนาเดียวครองรัฐ"},{"id":"nu-muh","front":"What do Nahdlatul Ulama and Muhammadiyah illustrate in Indonesia?","back":"Large Islamic organizations can be major civil-society and political influences ไทยช่วยจำ: องค์กรอิสลามใหญ่เป็น civil society ที่ทั้งต่อรอง บริการ และ mobilize ได้"},{"id":"malaysia","front":"How is Islam positioned in Malaysia?","back":"Islam is the religion of the Federation and is closely intertwined with Malay identity and party politics ไทยช่วยจำ: แข่งฐาน Malay-Muslim ทำให้พรรคมี incentive แสดงความ Islamic"},{"id":"thailand","front":"What religion-state pattern does Thailand illustrate?","back":"Buddhist-majority national identity with formal religious freedom and a politically important Malay-Muslim southern border ไทยช่วยจำ: ชายแดนใต้ = ศาสนา + ประวัติศาสตร์ + Malay identity + governance"},{"id":"philippines","front":"What dual pattern defines religion and politics in the Philippines?","back":"Strong Catholic influence nationally alongside long Muslim Moro struggles for recognition/autonomy in Mindanao ไทยช่วยจำ: BARMM = กลไก autonomy ตอบโจทย์ Moro identity/การปกครอง"},{"id":"church-ph","front":"How can the Catholic Church influence Philippine politics without governing directly?","back":"Through moral authority, public advocacy, mediation and mobilization ไทยช่วยจำ: Church network + moral authority ช่วย frame/mobilize protest"},{"id":"myanmar","front":"What makes Buddhism politically complex in Myanmar?","back":"Monks and Buddhist identity have supported both pro-democracy activism and exclusionary nationalism ไทยช่วยจำ: ศาสนาเดียวถูกใช้ทั้ง pro-democracy และ nationalist exclusion ได้"},{"id":"rohingya","front":"What political issue is central to the Rohingya case?","back":"A Muslim minority faces discrimination/statelessness where religion, ethnicity and citizenship exclusion overlap ไทยช่วยจำ: ไม่มี citizenship → ขาดการคุ้มครอง/สิทธิจากรัฐอย่างรุนแรง"},{"id":"brunei","front":"What is Brunei’s Melayu Islam Beraja (MIB)?","back":"Malay Islamic Monarchy—an ideology fusing Malay identity, Islam and royal authority ไทยช่วยจำ: monarchy + Malay + Islam รวมกันในอุดมการณ์รัฐ"},{"id":"socialist","front":"How do Laos and Vietnam broadly manage religion?","back":"They tolerate recognized religious practice under substantial socialist state supervision and regulation ไทยช่วยจำ: รับศาสนาเป็น culture ได้ พร้อมควบคุมองค์กรอิสระ"}]},{"id":"tu6-l6-democracy-v1","label":"Part 4 • Democracy, Populism, Minority Rights & State Control","description":"Secular/religious models, civic action, majoritarianism, populism and future dilemmas.","cards":[{"id":"spectrum","front":"What does the lecture mean by a spectrum from secular to religious politics?","back":"States vary in how directly religion is embedded in law, identity and governance ไทยช่วยจำ: ส่วนใหญ่ผสม ไม่ใช่ secular/theocracy สุดขั้ว"},{"id":"secular","front":"What is secular politics in the lecture’s ideal-type definition?","back":"State neutrality or institutional separation between religious authority and political rule ไทยช่วยจำ: ไม่เป็น theocracy ไม่ได้แปลว่า state neutral; บางรัฐควบคุมศาสนาเข้ม"},{"id":"relig-dem","front":"How can religion support democratization?","back":"Religious values, networks and leaders can mobilize justice, participation and resistance to authoritarianism ไทยช่วยจำ: network ศาสนาเป็นโครงสร้าง mobilize เมื่อช่องการเมืองปกติแคบ"},{"id":"majoritarian","front":"What is religious majoritarianism?","back":"Using the majority faith as a basis for privileging one political identity over minorities ไทยช่วยจำ: เสียงข้างมากไม่ควรลบสิทธิเท่าเทียมของ minority"},{"id":"pop-symbol","front":"How do populist leaders use religion?","back":"They invoke religious symbols and moral language to claim closeness with 'the people' ไทยช่วยจำ: ทำ policy disagreement ให้เป็น moral identity conflict"},{"id":"polarization","front":"What is a risk of religious populism?","back":"Polarization and weakening of pluralism/minority rights ไทยช่วยจำ: ถ้ามองฝ่ายตรงข้ามเป็นศัตรูไร้ legitimacy กติกากลาง/compromise อยู่ยาก"},{"id":"minority","front":"What recurring challenge links Thailand’s south, Rohingya and Moro cases?","back":"Balancing majority national identity with minority recognition, rights and political inclusion ไทยช่วยจำ: equal citizenship + recognition/voice ของ minority"},{"id":"transnational","front":"What future influence can reshape domestic religious politics?","back":"Transnational religious movements and geopolitical networks ไทยช่วยจำ: network/idea/funding ศาสนาข้ามพรมแดนทำให้ politics ภายในเชื่อมโลก"},{"id":"balance","front":"What is the central future dilemma in the lecture conclusion?","back":"Balancing religious identity, pluralism, rights and political stability ไทยช่วยจำ: ให้ศาสนามีส่วนร่วมได้ แต่ต้องคุ้มครอง equal citizenship และกัน exclusion"},{"id":"not-fixed","front":"What is the best overall conclusion about religion’s political effect?","back":"It is not fixed: religion can legitimate rulers, resist them, support democracy or fuel exclusion depending on context ไทยช่วยจำ: อย่าติดป้ายศาสนาว่า pro/anti democracy แบบถาวร; ดู actor/context"}]}]}};window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("d-study-tu101-module-loaded",{detail:{id:"l6"}}));})();